  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "## Frequency summary\n\nAmong the named miRNAs in the retrieved records, **miR-214 is the most frequently linked**, appearing in two records; **miR-15b, miR-1224-5p, miR-582-3p, and miR-218** each appear in one retrieved record.\n\n## Direct evidence\n\n- **miR-214 — most recurrent:** In aged human fracture specimens, elevated miR-214 correlated with lower bone formation, while mouse and in-vitro osteoblast experiments showed that miR-214 inhibited bone formation by directly targeting ATF4 [PMID: 23223004].\n- **miR-15b:** In an ovariectomy-induced osteoporosis mouse model and osteoblast experiments, miR-15b was elevated and inhibited osteoblast differentiation and autophagy through USP7/KDM6B regulation [PMID: 33434305].\n- **miR-1224-5p:** In vitro and mouse studies showed that miR-1224-5p promoted osteoblast differentiation, suppressed osteoclast differentiation, and ameliorated osteoporosis through ADCY2/Rap1 signaling [PMID: 35831436].\n- **miR-582-3p:** In osteoporosis tissues and C2C12 osteogenic-differentiation experiments, miR-582-3p was increased, inhibited osteoblast markers, and targeted HOXA10 [PMID: 35285389].\n\n## Association\n\n- **miR-218:** A review identifies miR-218 as temporally upregulated during osteoblastogenesis, but the supplied record does not provide a specific osteoporosis experiment for miR-218 [PMID: 23605904].\n\n## Testable hypothesis\n\nThe retrieved evidence supports testing **miR-214 inhibition** as the most established miRNA-centered anabolic strategy for osteoporosis, while miR-15b inhibition, miR-1224-5p augmentation, and miR-582-3p inhibition remain additional model-supported candidates [PMID: 23223004; PMID: 33434305; PMID: 35831436; PMID: 35285389].\n\n## Graph-derived candidate insights\n\nNo graph-derived candidate insight is reported: the only supplied path, **PATH 65e20457af215c29**, is **GATE TIER B; RETRIEVAL-ONLY; NOT-CLAIM-SAFE**, so it cannot support a graph claim.",
    "traditional_rag": "## Most frequently linked miRNA\n\n**miR-214** is the most frequently recurring specific miRNA in the retrieved literature:\n\n- It is directly associated with reduced bone formation in **human osteoporotic fracture specimens**; elevated miR-214 correlated with lower bone formation. In mouse osteoporosis models, miR-214 inhibited osteoblast activity and mineralization by targeting **ATF4**. [PMID: 23223004]\n- It is also cited in a review of lncRNA–miRNA interactions in osteogenesis, including **MALAT1/miR-214** and **KCNQ1OT1/miR-214**. [PMID: 34311172]\n\nThus, miR-214 has the strongest combination of **repeated citation, osteoporosis relevance, and direct mechanistic evidence** in the supplied records.\n\n## Other repeatedly or prominently linked miRNAs\n\nThe following were each identified in one retrieved record as osteoporosis- or osteogenesis-related:\n\n- **miR-15b** — increased in osteoporotic mice; silencing improved bone mineral density and mechanical properties, while overexpression inhibited osteoblast differentiation and autophagy through the **USP7/KDM6B** axis. **Animal and in-vitro evidence.** [PMID: 33434305]\n- **miR-1224-5p** — promoted osteoblast differentiation, reduced osteoclast differentiation, and ameliorated osteoporosis in estrogen-deficient and aged mouse models through **ADCY2/Rap1** signaling. **In-vitro and animal evidence.** [PMID: 35831436]\n- **miR-582-3p** — upregulated in osteoporosis tissues and inhibited osteoblast markers by targeting **HOXA10**. **Tissue and in-vitro evidence; species is not specified in the abstract.** [PMID: 35285389]\n- **miR-218** — highlighted in a review as temporally upregulated during osteoblastogenesis. [PMID: 23605904]\n- **miR-30, miR-24-3p, miR-33a, and miR-140-5p** — mentioned in lncRNA–miRNA regulatory interactions related to osteogenesis, but the supplied abstract does not establish osteoporosis-specific evidence for them. [PMID: 34311172]\n\n## Evidence interpretation\n\n- **Direct evidence:** miR-214, miR-15b, miR-1224-5p, and miR-582-3p have experimental links to osteoblast differentiation or bone formation in the supplied records. [PMID: 23223004; PMID: 33434305; PMID: 35831436; PMID: 35285389]\n- **Association/review evidence:** miR-218 and miR-148a are cited as differentiation-associated miRNAs, but miR-148a is linked primarily to osteoclastogenesis rather than osteogenesis. [PMID: 23605904]\n- **Testable therapeutic hypothesis:** inhibiting miR-214, miR-15b, or miR-582-3p, or augmenting miR-1224-5p, may improve osteoblast-mediated bone formation in osteoporosis; the supplied literature does not establish clinical efficacy in humans. [PMID: 23223004; PMID: 33434305; PMID: 35831436; PMID: 35285389]\n\n**Overall ranking from the supplied records:** **miR-214** is the clearest and most frequently linked candidate, followed by **miR-15b, miR-1224-5p, and miR-582-3p** as experimentally supported but less frequently recurring candidates.",
    "general_llm": "Without access to the specific retrieved set, the miRNAs most repeatedly associated in the broader osteoporosis/osteogenic-differentiation literature are approximately the following:\n\n| miRNA | Commonly reported relationship to osteogenesis/osteoporosis |\n|---|---|\n| **miR-21** | Generally pro-osteogenic; promotes osteoblast differentiation and can suppress inhibitors such as **SMAD7** and **PTEN**, although effects are context-dependent. |\n| **miR-29a/b/c** | Among the most consistently studied pro-osteogenic miRNAs; supports extracellular-matrix production and mineralization, partly through regulation of collagen and Wnt-related inhibitors. |\n| **miR-2861** | Strongly linked to osteoblast differentiation and bone formation; targets **HDAC5** and is often discussed as an osteoblast-specific or osteogenesis-associated miRNA. |\n| **miR-133a** | Frequently associated with reduced osteogenesis; inhibits osteoblast differentiation through targets including **RUNX2**. |\n| **miR-34a** | Usually anti-osteogenic and associated with age-related bone loss; can inhibit osteoblast proliferation/differentiation and promote osteoclast-related effects in some models. |\n| **miR-204/miR-211** | Commonly reported anti-osteogenic miRNAs that suppress **RUNX2** and impair osteoblast differentiation. |\n| **miR-138** | Generally inhibits osteoblast differentiation and bone formation, often through effects on **FAK**, **Sox9**, or related signaling pathways. |\n| **miR-100** | Frequently linked to impaired osteogenesis and altered mTOR/Wnt signaling; often described as increased in osteoporotic or poorly osteogenic settings. |\n| **miR-196a** | Usually pro-osteogenic; promotes osteoblast differentiation through regulation of **HOX** genes and related osteogenic pathways. |\n| **miR-218** | Recurrently identified as pro-osteogenic, particularly through enhancement of **Wnt/β-catenin** signaling. |\n| **miR-335-5p** | Associated with osteoblast differentiation and bone formation; reported to regulate **RUNX2**-related networks and Wnt signaling. |\n| **miR-17-5p / miR-106b** | Often reported as pro-osteogenic in mesenchymal stem cells, although their effects vary with cellular context and target expression. |\n| **miR-27a/b** | Frequently linked to promotion of osteoblast differentiation and regulation of adipogenic–osteogenic lineage allocation. |\n| **miR-30 family, especially miR-30a/c** | Commonly described as anti-osteogenic through repression of osteogenic transcription factors such as **RUNX2**. |\n\n### Most consistently recurring group\n\nIf the question is asking for a short list of the miRNAs most often encountered across osteoporosis-related osteogenesis studies, the strongest candidates are:\n\n**miR-21, miR-29a, miR-2861, miR-133a, miR-34a, miR-204/211, miR-138, miR-100, miR-196a, miR-218, and miR-335-5p.**\n\nThe evidence is mainly from cell and animal models. Importantly, a miRNA’s reported direction can differ between studies because of differences in osteoblasts versus mesenchymal stem cells, disease stage, aging, mechanical loading, and whether the study examines osteoblast formation, osteoclast activity, or circulating miRNA biomarkers. Thus, “associated with osteoporosis” does not always mean that the miRNA directly controls osteogenesis."
